      "content": "You are completely right about the first term: $2^{x-3} \\div 2^{x-3} = 1$.\n\nFor the second term, remember your exponent law for division: $\\frac{a^m}{a^n} = a^{m-n}$. If you divide $3 \\cdot 2^{x+1}$ by your common factor $2^{x-3}$, what do you get when you subtract the exponents $(x+1) - (x-3)$?"
